{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "kind": "lesson",
  "items": [
    {
      "external_id": "C11-PHY-CH7-7.6-CASE-2-MODES-OF-VIBRATION-IN-AN-ORGAN-PIPE-CLOSED-AT-ONE-END",
      "chapter": "pk-punjab/xi/physics/c11-phy/ch7",
      "topic": "pk-punjab/xi/physics/c11-phy/ch7/u-case-2-modes-of-vibration-in-an-organ-pipe-closed-at-one-end",
      "title": "Case (2): Modes of vibration in an organ pipe closed at one end",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "e2c5c034-041b-5340-9823-5e3c8c88a07c",
          "pdf_from": 150,
          "pdf_to": 155,
          "note": "Case (2): Modes of vibration in an organ pipe closed at one end: a main subheading of 7.6 Stationary waves in air columns, its own page (owner 2026-11-04); PDF 150-155."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Let us consider an organ pipe of length l which is closed at one end. Then at the closed end, we get a node while at the open end, we get an antinode as shown in the Fig. 7.9."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "(a) Fundamental mode of vibration:"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Fundamental mode of vibration has one node and one antinode as shown in Fig. 7.9 (a). If λ₁ is the wavelength of fundamental mode then length of the pipe is:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ell = \\frac{\\lambda_1}{4}",
            "text_alt": "l = λ₁/4"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{or} \\quad \\lambda_1 = 4\\ell \\qquad (7.10)",
            "text_alt": "or λ₁ = 4l   (7.10)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "So, the speed v becomes"
          },
          {
            "type": "diagram",
            "$asset": "c11-phy-ch7-fig-closed-pipe",
            "alt": "Three pipes open at the left and closed at the right, with shaded displacement envelopes. (a) l = lambda1/4, f1 = v/4l; (b) l = 3(lambda3/4), f3 = 3(v/4l); (c) l = 5(lambda5/4), f5 = 5(v/4l). Above each, green double arrows mark the antinodes, the first at the open end, and dots the nodes, the last at the closed end.",
            "caption": "Figure 7.9: Stationary longitudinal waves in a pipe closed at one end. Only odd harmonics are present (original drawing after the textbook's Figure 7.9).",
            "side": true
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "v = f_1\\lambda_1",
            "text_alt": "v = f₁λ₁"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "f_1 = \\frac{v}{\\lambda_1}",
            "text_alt": "f₁ = v/λ₁"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "f_1 = \\frac{v}{4\\ell} \\qquad (\\because \\lambda_1 = 4\\ell)",
            "text_alt": "f₁ = v/4l   (∵ λ₁ = 4l)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The frequency f₁ is called fundamental frequency."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "(b) Second Mode of Vibration:"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Second mode of vibration contains two nodes and two anti-nodes as shown in Fig. 7.9 (b)."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "note",
            "title": "A Scientific Fact",
            "text": "In an organ pipe, the primary driving mechanism is wavering, sheet like jet of air from flute-slit, which interacts with the upper lip and the air column in the pipe to maintain a steady oscillation."
          },
          {
            "type": "diagram",
            "$asset": "c11-phy-ch7-fig-vis7-6-organ-pipe-jet",
            "alt": "A vertical organ pipe (orange walls, yellow inside) on a tapered foot. Air (arrow at the bottom) rises through the foot, flows along the curved face of a dark block and leaves through the narrow flue-slit at the mouth on the left. Above the mouth the sheet-like jet wavers about the upper lip, curling into eddies alternately outside and inside the pipe; a small arrow at the top left points upward.",
            "caption": "A Scientific Fact (PDF page 153): the wavering, sheet-like air jet from the flue-slit of an organ pipe (original drawing after the textbook's picture in this box).",
            "side": true
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "If λ₃ is the wavelength, then length of the pipe is:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ell = \\frac{\\lambda_3}{4} + \\frac{\\lambda_3}{2}",
            "text_alt": "l = λ₃/4 + λ₃/2"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ell = \\frac{3}{4}\\lambda_3",
            "text_alt": "l = (3/4) λ₃"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\lambda_3 = \\frac{4\\ell}{3} \\qquad (7.11)",
            "text_alt": "λ₃ = 4l/3   (7.11)"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "f_3 = \\frac{v}{\\lambda_3}",
            "text_alt": "f₃ = v/λ₃"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Putting value of λ₃, we have   v = f₃λ₃"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "f_3 = \\frac{v}{4\\ell/3}",
            "text_alt": "f₃ = v/(4l/3)"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{or} \\quad f_3 = \\frac{3v}{4\\ell}",
            "text_alt": "or f₃ = 3v/4l"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{so} \\quad f_3 = 3f_1 \\qquad \\left[\\because \\frac{v}{4\\ell} = f_1\\right]",
            "text_alt": "so f₃ = 3f₁   [∵ v/4l = f₁]"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "This is called second harmonic.",
            "book_errors": [
              "second harmonic"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "correction",
            "title": "Book correction",
            "text": "f₃ = 3f₁ is the third harmonic, since the harmonic number is f/f₁ = 3. It is the second mode of vibration of a pipe closed at one end, which has only odd harmonics (as the caption of Fig. 7.9 says)."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "(c) nth mode of vibration"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "If air column vibration in n loops, then frequency fₙ is:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "f_n = n\\left(\\frac{v}{4\\ell}\\right)",
            "text_alt": "fₙ = n (v/4l)"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "f_n = nf_1",
            "text_alt": "fₙ = nf₁"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "and the wavelength λₙ is:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\lambda_n = \\frac{4\\ell}{n} \\qquad (7.12) \\qquad \\text{where } n = 1, 3, 5, \\dots",
            "text_alt": "λₙ = 4l/n   (7.12)   where n = 1, 3, 5, …"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "By studying both cases, we conclude that the pipe which is open at both ends is richer in harmonics than that of closed at one end."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "Example 7.4"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "An organ pipe has a length of 50 cm. Find the frequency of its fundamental note and the next harmonic, when it is:"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "(a) open at both ends"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "(b) closed at one end"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "(speed of sound = 350 m s⁻¹)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Solution: l = 50 cm = (50/100) m = 0.5 m",
            "bold": [
              "Solution:"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "v = 350 m s⁻¹"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "a) When pipe is open at both ends:",
            "bold": [
              "a) When pipe is open at both ends:"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Fundamental frequency   f₁ = ?"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Next harmonic frequency   f₂ = ?"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The frequency for nth harmonic in an open organ pipe is:"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "fₙ = n (v/2l)   when n = 1, 2, 3, ………"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "so the fundamental frequency is,"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "f₁ = (1 × 350)/(2 × 0.5)   put n = 1"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "f₁ = 350 Hz"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Next harmonic frequency i.e., n = 2 is:"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "f₂ = 2v/2l"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "f₂ = v/l = 350/0.5"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "f₂ = 700 Hz"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "b) When pipe is closed at one end:",
            "bold": [
              "b) When pipe is closed at one end:"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Fundamental frequency   f₁ = ?"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Next harmonic frequency   f₃ = ?"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "When the pipe is closed at one end then frequency for nth harmonic is:"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "fₙ = n (v/4l)   when n = 1, 3, 5, 7, ………"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "So fundamental frequency is:"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "f₁ = (1 × 350)/(4 × 0.5)   put n = 1"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "f₁ = 175 Hz"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Next harmonic frequency i.e., n = 3 is:"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "f₃ = 3v/4l"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "f₃ = (3 × 350)/(4 × 0.5)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "f₃ = 525 Hz"
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "note",
            "title": "Ponder Upon",
            "text": "Open pipes produce all harmonics, while closed pipes produced only odd harmonics."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "Example 7.5"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "A church organ consists of pipes, each open at one end, of different lengths. The minimum length is 30 mm and the longest is 4 m. Calculate the frequency range of the fundamental notes. (speed of sound = 340 m s⁻¹)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Solution: l_min = 30 mm = (30/1000) m = 30 × 10⁻³ m",
            "bold": [
              "Solution:"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "l_max = 4 m"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "v = 340 m s⁻¹"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Frequency range = ?"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "For an organ pipe open at one end only:"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "fₙ = nv/4l"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "(i) Minimum length",
            "bold": [
              "(i) Minimum length"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "For fundamental frequency, put n = 1"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "f₁,max = nv/4l_min"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "f₁,max = (1 × 340)/(4 × 30 × 10⁻³)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "f₁,max = 2833.33 Hz"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "(ii) Maximum length:",
            "bold": [
              "(ii) Maximum length:"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "For fundamental frequency, put n = 1"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "f₁,min = nv/4l_max"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "f₁,min = (1 × 340)/(4 × 4)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "f₁,min = 21.25 Hz"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "So, the fundamental frequency range is approximately from 21 Hz to 2833 Hz."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "note",
            "title": "Brain Teaser",
            "text": "A flute player notices that the flute is producing a pitch which is slightly sharp. What could be the cause of this problem?"
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
